## <%if .DisplayName%><%.DisplayName%><%else%><%.Slug%><%end%> — shape: `group goal(condition, cap)`.
##
## A goal is a CONDITION plus a CAP, and the thing that decides it is a
## command — never the model's opinion of its own work. Check (a tool,
## deterministic) → done when it exits 0, or advance (the agent) and check
## again, at most `goal_cap` times, then a typed failure. The pair is a
## `group`, so one bot can carry several goals (`use goal as deploy with
## { … }`, `use goal as migrate with { … }`) without copying the cluster:
## a group is a compile-time macro, so nothing of it reaches the runtime.
##
## Why the judge is a command: an evaluator that reads the transcript of
## the agent it judges is gameable, and the agent has no way to make the
## world true by writing that it is. `goal_condition` runs in the
## workspace and its EXIT CODE is the verdict; its output is carried for
## the next pass to read, not for the verdict. Fill in: the mission,
## `goal_condition`, `goal_cap`.
##
## docs/groups-iteration-subbots.md

dsl: 2

<%template "vars_block" .%>
## met is the check's exit code, not a field a model fills: the only
## writer of this schema is the tool below.
schema goal_state:
  met: bool
  log: string

schema advance_out:
  summary: string

prompt mission:
<%indent 2 .Instructions%>

prompt advance_user:
  The goal is not reached yet. This is what the check reported:
  ```
  {{outputs.reach.check.log}}
  ```
  Make the check pass. Change the workspace, not the report: the same
  command runs again after you, and its exit code is the only verdict.
  Say in summary what you changed and why you expect the check to pass.

group goal(condition, cap):
  ## No LLM: the operator's own command, run in the workspace. A `{{ref}}`
  ## in a command is shell-escaped by the runtime, and a group's
  ## `{{params.X}}` is substituted at COMPILE time — so what lands here is
  ## the ref the `use` binds, escaped once when the node runs.
  ##
  ## An EMPTY condition is refused by name rather than treated as met: a
  ## goal whose check is `bash -c ''` would exit 0 on the first pass and
  ## report a reached goal nobody checked. jq's absence fails the node the
  ## same way — without the guard, `$(… | jq …)` would substitute nothing,
  ## the stdout would not parse, and the run would end on a verdict that
  ## was never computed. jq is pinned in the bundle's devbox.json and
  ## ships in every iterion sandbox image.
  tool check:
    command: `test -n {{params.condition}} || { echo "goal: the stop condition is empty — a goal with no check is met by default" >&2; exit 1; }; command -v jq >/dev/null || { echo "goal: jq is required to JSON-encode the check output" >&2; exit 1; }; out=$(bash -c {{params.condition}} 2>&1); rc=$?; met=false; [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && met=true; printf '%s' "$out" | jq -Rs --argjson met "$met" '{met: $met, log: .[-4000:]}' || { echo "goal: jq failed to encode the check output" >&2; exit 1; }`
    output: goal_state
  agent advance:<%template "llm_fields_nested" .%>
    system: mission
    user: advance_user
    output: advance_out
  advance -> check
  ## The exit is guarded on the condition the back-edge negates, so it
  ## cannot pre-empt the loop on the first pass. The bare edge last is the
  ## loop-exhaustion exit: taken when the back-edge is declined.
  check -> done when met
  check -> advance when not met as goal_loop("{{params.cap}}")
  check -> goal_not_reached

use goal as reach with {
  condition: "{{vars.goal_condition}}",
  cap: "{{vars.goal_cap}}"
}

fail goal_not_reached:
  description: "the goal's check never passed within the cap"
  code: GOAL_NOT_REACHED
  message: "the check still fails after {{vars.goal_cap}} attempts: {{outputs.reach.check.log}}"

workflow <%.WorkflowName%>:
  entry: reach.check<%template "worktree_dial" .%><%template "dials" .%><%template "budget_block" .%>
